      "rtCritic": 90,
      "metacritic": 70,
      "rangeLow": 135000000,
      "rangeHigh": 145000000,
      "analysis": "Friday actuals are in: $43M, putting weekend two on pace for Deadline's evolving $135M-$143M estimate — a touch under Box Office Pro's pre-weekend $150M-$160M call but still the third-best domestic second weekend of all time, trailing only The Force Awakens ($149.2M) and Endgame ($147.4M) and clearing Infinity War's $114.8M third-place mark. That pushes the domestic cume from $510M heading into the weekend past $650M by Sunday, with worldwide already at $1.216B as of Friday. The bigger headline: at $510M+ domestic pre-weekend, Brand New Day has now passed Toy Story 5's running $470.8M total to become the outright #1 domestic release of 2026, not just #2.",
      "rulerLow": 125000000,
      "rulerHigh": 160000000,
      "currentEstimate": 140000000,
      "estimateColorMode": "gradient",
      "estimateColor": "#5C8FF0"
    },
    {
      "title": "One Night Only",
      "distributor": "Universal",
      "theaters": 3012,
      "confidence": "high",
      "momentum": "softening",
      "rtCritic": 51,
      "metacritic": null,
      "rangeLow": 5500000,
      "rangeHigh": 6500000,
      "analysis": "Thursday previews landed soft at $750K, and Friday confirmed the trend: a $2.8M actual (third place, 3,012 theaters) has Deadline projecting a $6M weekend — landing between the two trackers' pre-weekend calls (Box Office Pro's $7M-$10M vs. Box Office Theory's $3M-$5.5M pinpoint) but much closer to Theory's read. Reviews came in mixed-to-soft at 51% on Rotten Tomatoes, consistent with the rom-com ceiling staying low this cycle (You, Me & Tuscany's $7.7M open, The Roses' $6.2M open). Lands well behind Brand New Day's second weekend, as expected.",
      "rulerLow": 4000000,
      "rulerHigh": 8000000,
